## Verification of the Interrupt Generator
##
## The module is checked at a 4-bit address (only the top bit is decoded, so the
## width is irrelevant to the logic) and 4-bit data, in two depths.
##
## G_MAX_OUTSTANDING=2 is the shipping configuration -- what test/system.vhd
## instantiates and what both masters in this CPU cap themselves at.
##
## G_MAX_OUTSTANDING=3 is NOT redundant, and the reason is worth knowing. At a
## depth of 2 a per-slave response buffer never holds more than one word
## (f_buf0_bound in the .psl says why), so order WITHIN one slave is
## unreachable and a buffer that popped its entries backwards would pass every
## property. At 3 it is reachable, and f_cover_buf0_deep / f_cover_buf1_deep
## insist that it actually happens. Fault injection confirms the distinction:
## replacing buf0(0) with buf0(buf0_fill - 1) is caught only by the 3-deep
## tasks.
##
## The data width is 4 rather than 16 because wb_mux.psl tags responses in the
## top and bottom data bits and ignores the rest; narrowing the payload cuts the
## state space without weakening a single property.
##
## Only bmc and cover are in the task list, as in memory.sby. Every assertion
## except f_response_order also closes under k-induction -- adding a "prove"
## task and commenting that one property out passes basecase and induction
## both -- but f_response_order itself does not, for the reason written above
## it in wb_mux.psl. Adding "prove" here as it stands would simply make
## "make -C formal" fail.
##
## Run one task:   sby -f interrupt.sby bmc
## Run everything: sby -f interrupt.sby

[tasks]
bmc    bmc
cover  cover

[options]
bmc:   mode bmc
bmc:   depth 10
cover: mode cover
cover: depth 10

[engines]
smtbmc

[script]
ghdl --std=08 interrupt.vhd interrupt.psl -e interrupt
prep -top interrupt

[files]
interrupt.psl
../test/interrupt.vhd

